tiles.body:
Each city works the 21 tiles of its fat cross: a 5 × 5 block with the four corners cut off. The city tile is always worked for free, and every citizen either works one more tile or serves as a specialist. A tile can only be worked if it lies inside your borders, is not already worked by another city and holds no foreign units.

Tiles yield food (growth), shields (production) and commerce (gold, science and luxuries). The terrain sets the base: Grassland 2 food, Plains 1 food and 1 shield, Forest 1 food and 2 shields, Coast 1 food and 2 commerce. On top of that come:
- resources on the tile;
- a river along any of its edges, +1 commerce;
- a road, +1 commerce on most land;
- irrigation, +1 food, or a mine, +1 shield (+2 on hills and mountains);
- a railroad, +1 shield where the tile already yields shields.

The city tile counts as irrigated where its terrain allows it and always makes at least 1 shield.

Government and buildings adjust yields last. Under [Despotism](pedia:governments/despotism) and [Anarchy](pedia:governments/anarchy) any yield above 2 loses one point unless the city is celebrating. The [Republic](pedia:governments/republic) and [Democracy](pedia:governments/democracy) add 1 commerce to tiles that already make some. A [Harbor](pedia:buildings/harbor) adds 1 food on water tiles and an [Offshore Platform](pedia:buildings/offshorePlatform) 1 shield.

Unless you place citizens by hand, a city places its own: food first while it would grow too slowly, then a balance of food, shields and commerce.

happiness.body:
Only citizens working tiles have a mood; specialists have none. Each city starts with a number of content citizens set by the difficulty (4 at Chieftain, 2 at Regent, 1 from Emperor up), one fewer for each optimal-city-number of cities in your empire. Everyone else is born unhappy. Then, in order:
- forced labour and war weariness turn content citizens unhappy;
- each connected kind of luxury resource gives a happy face, more with a [Marketplace](pedia:buildings/marketplace);
- each point of luxury spending gives a happy face (+50% with a Marketplace), and each entertainer adds one;
- buildings make citizens content: [Temple](pedia:buildings/temple) 1, [Colosseum](pedia:buildings/colosseum) 2, [Cathedral](pedia:buildings/cathedral) 3, plus several wonders;
- military police: each military unit in the city calms one unhappy citizen, up to 2 under Despotism, 3 under Monarchy and Feudalism, 4 under Communism and Fascism, and none under the Republic and Democracy.

A happy face turns a content citizen happy. On an unhappy citizen, one face makes it content and a second makes it happy.

A city with more unhappy than happy citizens falls into disorder: no shields, no tax, no science, no luxury and no stored food, though a deficit still drains the box. Cities that place their own citizens turn workers into entertainers to prevent it.

A city of size 6 or more with no unhappy citizens, more happy than content citizens and no food deficit celebrates We Love the King Day. While it lasts the Despotism tile penalty is lifted and waste is halved.

cultureFlips.body:
Conquered people do not always accept new masters. When you capture a city, each citizen may resist, with a chance set by how its people see your culture: 40% if they are in awe of you, up to 90% if they are disdainful. Retaking a city of your own people meets no resistance. While anyone resists, the city produces no shields, gold, science or luxury and does not grow. Each turn resisters may give up, and every two military land units in the city quell one more.

Cities can also change sides on their own. Each civilization's influence on a city is the sum, over its cities within 8 tiles, of (city culture + 10 × size) ÷ distance, with the city itself counting at distance 1. When the strongest foreign influence is at least twice the home influence, the city may flip each turn. The chance is:
- (influence ratio − 100%) ÷ 50, plus a fifth of the culture table's propaganda value for that civilization;
- +5 if the city is in disorder, +5 if it has resisters, +5 if its people were born to that civilization;
- −2 for each military land unit inside;
clamped between 0 and 20% a turn.

Capitals never flip. A flipped city keeps its buildings except small wonders, and the garrison that failed to hold it is lost.

The city screen shows the odds and their reasons; happy, garrisoned border cities rarely flip. Flips can be switched off in the game options.

combat.body:
Moving onto a tile held by an enemy you are at war with is an attack. It costs one movement point, and a unit attacks only once a turn unless it has blitz.

Each unit has hit points from its experience: Conscript 2, Regular 3, Veteran 4, Elite 5, plus any bonus of its type. Each round the attacker wins with chance A ÷ (A + D), where A is its attack and D the defender's modified defence, and the loser of the round loses one hit point. There is no firepower: a lost round always costs one hit point. The battle ends when a side has none left.

The defender's bonuses are added together:
- terrain: +10% on flat land, +25% in forest and jungle, +50% on hills, +100% on mountains;
- +25% when the attack crosses a river, +50% in a fortress, +25% when fortified or inside a city;
- against land attacks on a city of size 7 to 12, +50%, on a metropolis +100%; [City Walls](pedia:buildings/walls) give a town +50%;
- a [Coastal Fortress](pedia:buildings/coastalFortress) adds +50% against ships.

The best defender on the tile, by strength × hit points, fights; workers, settlers and artillery never defend.

A land unit faster than its opponent may withdraw instead of dying, with its level's chance: Conscript 34%, Regular 50%, Veteran 58%, Elite 66%. It survives with 1 hit point. Defenders never withdraw from cities or fortresses.

Resting units heal 1 hit point in the field, 2 in your own city, and fully with a [Barracks](pedia:buildings/barracks).

nukes.body:
Nuclear weapons exist only once someone, anywhere in the world, has completed the [Manhattan Project](pedia:buildings/manhattanProject). From then on any civilization with the right advances can build them:
- the [Tactical Nuke](pedia:units/tacticalNuke) strikes within 6 tiles of where it stands, and submarines can carry it closer;
- the [ICBM](pedia:units/icbm) strikes anywhere in the world.

A strike needs a target belonging to a civilization you are at war with, and the weapon is spent.

Missile defence: if a city within 3 tiles of the target belongs to a civilization with [Strategic Missile Defense](pedia:buildings/strategicMissileDefense), the missile is destroyed with a 75% chance.

When a weapon gets through, the blast covers the target and the eight tiles around it:
- every unit there is destroyed, whoever owns it, including your own;
- every city loses half its citizens, though at least one survives;
- land tiles are covered with fallout, which works like pollution until workers clean it;
- the target tile becomes a crater unless a city stands on it.

Every civilization hurt by the blast collects 10 war weariness points against the attacker. Fallout and craters add to global warming every turn they remain ([pollution](pedia:concepts/pollution)), so a nuclear war harms the whole world, not just its targets.

Against a nuclear-armed rival, Strategic Missile Defense in your border cities is the best insurance, and spreading your units out keeps one strike from destroying a whole army.

capture.body:
When your land unit wins the last battle for a tile, or walks onto a tile where nothing can defend, it moves in.

Units on the tile that cannot fight are dealt with by your unit's abilities. A unit able to capture takes Workers and artillery for your side and turns each Settler into two Workers; armies always can. Anything else, such as leaders, ships in port and aircraft, is destroyed.

Taking a city:
- you plunder its share of its owner's treasury: their gold × the city's size ÷ their total population;
- the city changes hands one citizen smaller, with an empty food box, shield box and build;
- the Palace and small wonders are lost, every other improvement survives with a 50% chance, and great wonders are kept;
- its people may resist your rule ([culture flips](pedia:concepts/cultureFlips)).

A size-1 city, or one you attack with the raze order, is destroyed instead, and its great wonders are lost for ever. On the turn you take a city you may also choose to raze it.

Slaves: some units, such as the [Javelin Thrower](pedia:units/javelinThrower), the [Man-O-War](pedia:units/manOWar) and the [Privateer](pedia:units/privateer), enslave the beaten, gaining a captive unit in 1 victory out of 3.

A civilization that loses its capital gets a free Palace in its largest remaining city. A civilization with no capital at all makes its next conquest the capital. A civilization left with no cities and no unit able to found one is eliminated, and its remaining units disappear.

warWeariness.body:
Long wars wear down people who have a voice in government. Your civilization keeps a count of war weariness points against every enemy:
- +1 for each battle with them;
- +3 for each unit lost to them;
- +10 for each city lost to them;
- +10 for being struck by their nuclear weapons.
Battles with barbarians never count.

Every turn the points against each enemy fall by a tenth, rounded up, and making peace clears them completely.

Only some governments feel it. Under the [Republic](pedia:governments/republic) and [Feudalism](pedia:governments/feudalism) weariness is low: every 40 points turn one content citizen unhappy in every city. Under [Democracy](pedia:governments/democracy) it is high: one citizen per 20 points. Despotism, Monarchy, Communism and Fascism ignore it completely.

A [Police Station](pedia:buildings/policeStation) halves war weariness in its city, and [Universal Suffrage](pedia:buildings/universalSuffrage) halves it in every city. The city screen shows war weariness as its own step in the citizens' moods, so you can see exactly what the war is costing.

In practice a democracy can fight short, decisive wars, but a long war of attrition slowly pushes its cities into disorder. Losing units hurts three times as much as merely fighting, so strike with overwhelming force. Raising the luxury rate, building police stations, or making peace for a while and letting the points fade are all ways to recover before the next campaign.
